<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="font-size:12.0pt;color:#2F5597">Eurasia
Group’s weekly roundup of top stories</span></b></span><span class="eop"><span style="font-size:12.0pt;color:#2F5597">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l8 level1 lfo4;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">eg&nbsp;update</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;from
     Ian Bremmer (05.04.23) | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
     &quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14595_a8955a96/wfpub_eurasia_14595_a8955a96.html/ec49aca6-6282-4ac8-87ca-b8fe4384f52e" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="normaltextrun">&nbsp;</span><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l8 level2 lfo4;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">This week, Ian shares the latest on Russia, namely the
      biggest headline regarding&nbsp;Russia’s arrest of Wall Street Journal
      reporter Evan Gershkovich on espionage charges. He talks about TikTok and
      technology being the fastest deteriorating piece of Us-China relations.
      With the indictment&nbsp;of Trump, the United States has made political
      history yet again. Finally, Israel hits pause on crisis.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><i><span style="color:black">Latin America</span></i></span><span class="eop"><span style="color:black">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:
9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Mexico:
     Treatment of Iberdrola will not extend to other companies in sector </span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.05.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14630_4762499c/wfpub_eurasia_14630_4762499c.html/f2b7e4c5-07b3-4f3e-885b-f28543ce0b26" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">On 4 April, power generator Iberdrola announced it
      will sell most of its Mexican assets to a private investment fund partly
      owned by Mexico’s National Infrastructure Fund (Fonadin); the Federal
      Electricity Commission (CFE) will become the assets’ new
      operator.&nbsp;President Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador’s administration has
      consistently targeted the Spanish company with criticism and harsh
      regulatory treatment; he believes it had received special treatment in
      the past and had direct connections to his political foes.&nbsp;The
      president wants to increase CFE’s role in the sector, but he is unlikely
      to push other companies out and will follow a more nuanced approach
      toward other investors.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Guatemala:&nbsp;Rios
     is early presidential favorite as official campaign gets underway&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.05.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14627_d142d493/wfpub_eurasia_14627_d142d493.html/bf7ec9cb-50c1-4567-9887-242905727de9" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Far-right candidate Zury Rios is the early
      presidential frontrunner ahead of the 25 June first-round presidential
      election; she will likely face&nbsp;center-left former first lady Sandra
      Torres in an August runoff, in which Rios would have an advantage owing
      to Torres’s high rejection rates. The exclusion of antiestablishment&nbsp;candidates
      Roberto Arzu and Thelma Cabrera from the race will deepen voter
      discontent and likely increase&nbsp;abstention; center-right Edmond Mulet
      could emerge&nbsp;as an alternative, but his candidacy is also in
      question. Rios and Torres represent broad continuity on economic policy
      and resistance to greater transparency; the latter issue will ensure
      persistent tensions with the US, though President Joe Biden’s
      administration will continue to focus on targeted sanctions.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Brazil:&nbsp;Laxer
     rules for sanitation contracts will affect private investment
     opportunities&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.05.23) | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14625_b3d0464b/wfpub_eurasia_14625_b3d0464b.html/bcb08df0-2f05-433a-96eb-d5780562aef5" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">At 4 pm (Brazil time) on 5 April, President Luiz
      Inacio Lula da Silva will sign two decrees reviewing regulations issued
      by the Jair Bolsonaro administration detailing the 2020 sanitation reform
      law. The revision will make it easier for state-owned water utilities to
      keep their current contracts, reducing pressure for privatizations. The
      administration will not pursue changes to the 2020 legislation, sparing
      the reform’s main pillars: mandatory tenders for new contracts and the 2033
      deadline to universalize coverage. Still, the upcoming specifications
      will allow state governors who do not want private partners to circumvent
      compulsory auctions and maintain&nbsp;their SOEs, despite the
      government’s encouragement of public-private partnerships (PPP).&nbsp; As
      Eurasia Group expected, the decrees will allow municipalities to hire
      PPPs with more flexibility by scrapping the 25% outsourcing limit.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Peru:&nbsp;Threats
     to Boluarte’s&nbsp;position will persist after failed motion to oust
     her&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.04.23) | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14622_85f09440/wfpub_eurasia_14622_85f09440.html/d05aa833-4a27-4325-9e56-e87d75362a1e" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">On 4 April, congress rejected an impeachment motion
      against President Dina Boluarte&nbsp;on the grounds of “permanent moral
      incapacity,” the first effort to oust Boluarte&nbsp;since she took office
      in December. But the government will remain extremely fragile and is
      unlikely to survive until July 2026. Protests have subsided and the
      attention of authorities recently shifted to responding to rains and
      flooding in several parts of the country. But public support for
      Boluarte&nbsp;and congress remains&nbsp;extremely low. The government
      will remain focused on its survival, and no meaningful reforms are likely
      to advance. Moreover, public discontent will persist in the long run,
      particularly if demands for early elections are ignored. Radical and
      antiestablishment&nbsp;candidates will probably be&nbsp;competitive in a
      crowded electoral field.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Brazil:&nbsp;Deal
     on first batch of provisional measures favors Lula’s agenda in
     congress&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.04.23) | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14616_cd509985/wfpub_eurasia_14616_cd509985.html/2a0d9c7d-8cb6-40de-94b4-42b06436affe" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">On 4 April, Institutional Relations Minister Alexandre
      Padilha announced that the administration struck a deal with both houses
      of parliament about the procedures to deliberate on the first 13
      provisional measures (MPs) enacted by President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva
      since 1 January. The arrangement does not solve the ongoing procedural
      dispute between Lira and Pacheco over provisional measures but eases the
      pressure concerning the first bills enacted by the Lula
      administration—which could expire between late May and early June. The
      deal clears the way for Lula’s early priorities, and the administration
      needs to have Lira and Pacheco reach a compromise over MPs procedures.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Brazil:&nbsp;Lessons
     from new fiscal framework and next steps&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.03.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14602_4b34a4c6/wfpub_eurasia_14602_4b34a4c6.html/4b58ef81-3b82-4d42-accd-1ee7222cccc5" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">The new fiscal framework represents&nbsp;a win for the
      economic team and suggests President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva is
      sensitive to the need to signal fiscal responsibility. The administration
      will not have much difficulty securing approval of its new fiscal anchor
      in congress by June or July, but it will achieve only partial success
      advancing the measures to be unveiled this week to boost revenue by BRL
      100 billion-150 billion reais given the competing need to bring the VAT
      reform to a vote in June. The fiscal rule, along with the likely
      approval&nbsp;of VAT reform, reinforces the country’s neutral short-term
      political trajectory; however, difficulties generating sufficient revenue
      to meet primary surplus targets, along with Lula’s poor reaction function
      if he loses popular support, are key risk factors to track.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Mexico:&nbsp;Morena’s
     push to gain control of INE’s board yields mixed results </span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(03.31.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14591_c4e0ecf3/wfpub_eurasia_14591_c4e0ecf3.html/db79c685-5368-42d0-a800-a90d8d5af02e" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">The lower house selected Guadalupe Taddei, who is
      close to the administration, as the new head of the National Electoral
      Institute (INE). Morena and the administration were unable to nominate
      loyalists to all four vacancies, however. The INE’s eleven-member board
      now has eight Morena-designated commissioners, out of which three have
      been highly independent and two of the incoming ones are unlikely to bend
      in favor of the administration. This process will not stop further
      attacks against the INE, as President Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador
      remains&nbsp;intent on decreasing as much as possible its leeway to
      monitor&nbsp;campaign spending and electoral violations. Thus, we expect
      further budget cuts down the line and a weaker board that will struggle
      to limit Morena’s electoral excesses in 2024.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo5;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Mexico:&nbsp;Central
     bank will remain focused on inflation with no political interference&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(03.31.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14588_faa1f596/wfpub_eurasia_14588_faa1f596.html/3642f777-1f1e-41fe-9cb0-d5d780206687" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l0 level2 lfo5;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">On 30 March, the central bank increased interest rates
      by 25 basis points. The board of governors is aware of
      perceptions&nbsp;of its weakness but will remain conservative, believing
      that it will take some time to achieve its 3% inflation target. President
      Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador has previously publicly questioned rate
      increases but is primarily concerned about inflation and will continue to
      respect <span style="background:#FFE5E5">Banxico’s</span>&nbsp;independence.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><i><span style="color:black">Europe</span></i></span><span class="eop"><span style="color:black">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:
9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level1 lfo6;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Poland:&nbsp;Opposition
     loses support, but its current travails may only be temporary&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.06.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14636_33ee1fd4/wfpub_eurasia_14636_33ee1fd4.html/18eec6ac-b433-46f5-8cd6-7d6d2abde896" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level2 lfo6;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Friction within the liberal opposition is causing a loss
      of support for pro-EU parties, while the “United Right” alliance of the
      conservative Law and Justice party (PiS) and its junior partner
      Solidarity Poland has arrested its decline in the polls. That said, this
      phenomenon might only be temporary; the likely unification&nbsp;of at
      least two liberal opposition parties in the coming weeks, and the
      potential nomination of Warsaw Mayor Rafal&nbsp;Trzaskowski&nbsp;as the
      opposition’s lead candidate instead of former prime minister Donald Tusk,
      could help the liberal opposition recuperate its lost support. The United
      Right will also face challenges in the coming weeks; for now, Eurasia
      Group is&nbsp;maintaining&nbsp;its 65% probability of a pro-EU, liberal
      opposition government&nbsp;emerging&nbsp;after the elections.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level1 lfo6;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">UK:&nbsp;Sunak
     is back in the race, but is unlikely to catch Labour&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.04.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14615_1cd29219/wfpub_eurasia_14615_1cd29219.html/66c9907a-ec32-4a93-a4d8-eb3346e56fbe" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level2 lfo6;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Having delivered a string of policy wins and restored
      unity to the Conservative Party, local elections on 4 May are no longer
      decisive for Prime Minister Rishi Sunak’s government—or his own fate. As
      such, Eurasia Group is reducing the odds of a Boris Johnson comeback to
      10% from 30%. Eurasia Group’s basecase for next year’s general
      elections—most likely in&nbsp;October 2024—remains&nbsp;a Labour majority
      (60% odds); we also assign a 30% probability to a hung parliament that
      results in a Labour minority government with informal support from the
      Liberal Democrats and/or the Scottish Nationalist Party and a 10%
      probability to a Tory majority.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level1 lfo6;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Germany,
     Sustainability:&nbsp;Tackling a phaseout of Russian fossil fuels while
     accelerating the green transition&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.04.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14611_f4b8fbcd/2023-04_germany-ecr_special_report.pdf/15684921-1f12-4598-900d-90fd8dd6ae73" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level2 lfo6;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Germany is attempting&nbsp;to phase out previously
      high Russian fossil fuel imports while enabling a green transition that
      continues to supply its heavy industry with reliable, affordable, and
      clean energy. The timeline for policy action is to first resolve the
      emergency by replacing all Russian fossil fuels with alternative
      suppliers (in 2022/2023), then move into a medium-term adaptation process
      by establishing the regulatory foundations to deploy the technologies
      needed to cut emissions (2023/2024); afterward, officials will launch the
      mass deployment of all decarbonized systems to enable clean and
      affordable energy supply, by 2030. The green energy and industry
      transition will require substantial energy and industrial government
      support programs at the national and EU levels, as well as deeper EU
      cooperation and global supply chain partnerships; it will likewise
      require some degree of green industrial protectionism.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level1 lfo6;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Russia-Ukraine
     Crisis&nbsp;Watch:&nbsp;New OPEC+ production cuts raise oil prices and
     provide&nbsp;an economic boost to Russia</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;(04.03.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14605_ac429490/wfpub_eurasia_14605_ac429490.html/71570f71-9071-4dc3-ba9c-ec7dabb1aa3d" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l7 level2 lfo6;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Surprise additional&nbsp;OPEC+ oil production cuts
      will bolster Moscow’s ability to withstand sanctions in the short term
      and potentially test Western ability to enforce its price cap. Recent
      Western military equipment deliveries to Ukraine, including Polish MiG-29
      fighter jets and German-designed Leopard tanks, continue to fuel
      speculation on timing of the expected Ukrainian counteroffensive; poor
      weather conditions in April are among the factors that suggest it is
      still some weeks away. Despite Russia’s arrest of US journalist Evan
      Gershkovich and a New START treaty data-sharing breakdown further raising
      tensions between Washington and Moscow, Eurasia Group’s scenarios and
      probabilities remain&nbsp;unchanged.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><i><span style="color:black">Asia</span></i></span><span class="eop"><span style="color:black">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:
9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l4 level1 lfo7;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Japan:&nbsp;New
     carbon trading system begins in April, but implementation will be
     gradual&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(03.31.23) | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14589_4c950f70/wfpub_eurasia_14589_4c950f70.html/9f483aa4-9f38-47de-a721-d7473648538d" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l4 level2 lfo7;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Japan announced its carbon pricing scheme would go
      into effect this April, to be implemented in stages between 2023 and
      2027. Since the carbon trading and pricing scheme will be implemented
      gradually, the short-term impact on emissions will be minimal. Impact on
      emissions by 2030 will depend on the pricing and enforcement measures,
      which are still under discussion. Japan's new carbon pricing system
      aligns with the timeline of the EU CBAM, which includes a tax period
      beginning in 2026, and represents&nbsp;another signal that the G7 members
      might be closer to formally aligning their climate policies.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><i><span style="color:black">North America</span></i></span><span class="eop"><span style="color:black">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:
9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l6 level1 lfo8;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Critical
     Minerals:&nbsp;Geopolitical turbulence will push North America to deepen
     supply chain integration and pursue new battery chemistries&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.03.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14604_ea953639/2023-04-03_critical_minerals_special_report_final.pdf/14dd53cd-38ed-442f-9c62-ce25b2f0f4f3" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l6 level2 lfo8;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">The following report is the first in a series from
      Eurasia Group’s new Critical Minerals Working Group, a cross-disciplinary
      assemblage of analysts from the following practices: Energy, Climate
      &amp; Resources; Industrials &amp; Supply Chains, and Geo-technology. It
      also leverages&nbsp;the work of specialists from regional and global
      macro practices. In coordination with Eurasia Group’s inaugural US-Canada
      Summit, this first report will focus on the role of CMs in North America.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><i><span style="color:black">Middle East and Africa</span></i></span><span class="eop"><span style="color:black">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:
9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l5 level1 lfo9;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">UAE:&nbsp;Authorities
     will seek&nbsp;to marginally reduce Russian economic ties&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.05.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14629_f7c933e4/wfpub_eurasia_14629_f7c933e4.html/b209204a-47da-4eb9-8a29-fc84ca22cc1e" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l5 level2 lfo9;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Given the risks that sanctions evasion could create,
      Emirati authorities will explore ways to reduce scrutiny of the country’s
      linkages with Russian entities. The UAE will likely seek&nbsp;to manage
      risks on the financial side and more carefully weigh the footprint of
      Russian financial institutions and companies in the economy; some
      curtailing of non-oil goods trade with Russia is also possible. However,
      any initiatives will remain modest and focused on risk/reputation
      management; significantly reducing the presence of Russian individuals or
      entities in the UAE is unlikely.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l5 level1 lfo9;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Saudi
     Arabia, Egypt:&nbsp;Sisi’s trip to Riyadh affirms strategic link and opens
     door to Arab normalization with Syria&nbsp;</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">(04.03.23)
     | Read </span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"><a href="https://library.eurasiagroup.net/document/open/wfpub_eurasia_14600_57e13764/wfpub_eurasia_14600_57e13764.html/4555a793-da68-4aa6-801c-3f699c218593" target="_blank"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="color:#0563C1;
     text-decoration:none;text-underline:none">here</span></span></a><span class="eop">&nbsp;</span></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l5 level2 lfo9;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Egyptian President Abdelfattah el&nbsp;Sisi’s meeting
      with Saudi Crown Prince Mohammad bin Salman on 2 April highlights the
      resilience of the strategic bond between the two countries. Saudi
      Arabia’s financial support to Egypt will require still more progress on
      reforms by Cairo that might prove challenging, but a withdrawal of
      assistance&nbsp;is unlikely. Riyadh is likely to normalize relations with
      Syria, and Sisi has received explicit support from Riyadh to also move
      toward normalization; Saudi Arabia’s regional initiatives are focused on
      easing regional tensions to improve its geopolitical influence and
      security environment.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>

<p class="paragraph" style="text-align:justify;background:white"><span class="normaltextrun"><i><span style="color:black">Sector Research</span></i></span><span class="eop"><span style="color:black">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="font-size:
9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif;color:black"></span></p>

<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;
     tab-stops:list .5in;background:white;user-select:text;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><b><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">Energy,
     Climate and Resources:</span></b></span><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;<b>Amid
     geopolitical and energy security concerns, technology breakthroughs will
     drive global green transition&nbsp;</b>(04.03.23)</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li><ul style="margin-top:0in" type="circle"><li class="paragraph" style="color:black;text-align:justify;mso-list:l3 level2 lfo10;
      tab-stops:list 1.0in;background:white;user-select:text;list-style-type:
      &quot;o  &quot;;cursor:text"><span class="normaltextrun"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">The energy transition is happening globally, but it
      will look different and progress at varying speeds in each country and
      region; geopolitical factors will play a role as will individual countries’
      economic circumstances, domestic energy resources, and politics.
      Technology breakthroughs are enabling more rapid decarbonization, with
      power and road transport moving more quickly along this path, while new
      possibilities are emerging&nbsp;for long-haul transport and heavy
      manufacturing. Tensions between emerging and industrialized markets will
      continue to grow as financing for energy transitions in lower-income
      countries remains&nbsp;insufficient and the costs of climate impacts are
      rising rapidly.</span></span><span class="eop"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;</span></span><span style="mso-fareast-font-family:
      &quot;Times New Roman&quot;"></span></li></ul></ul>]]></td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" height="50" >
<td tEditID="c1r3" style=" background-color:#FFFFFF; bEditID:r3st1; color:#000000; bLabel:main; font-size:12pt; font-family:arial;" aEditID="c1r3" locked="0" >
<![CDATA[]]></td>
</tr>
</table>